// ============================================================================
// collatz-peak.epher -- the highest value a Collatz sequence reaches
//
// Runs the Collatz sequence from a starting n and records the maximum
// value seen along the way (the flight height of the sequence). A packed
// while loop (guide 1.30) carries {current value, peak, steps}; the loop
// is silent, so one labelled print shows the peak. The classic checks:
// from 27 the sequence climbs to 9232 before falling to 1 in 111 steps,
// and from 13 it peaks at 40.
//
// Source: the Collatz conjecture, posed by Lothar Collatz in 1937; see
// Lagarias (ed.), "The Ultimate Challenge: The 3x+1 Problem", AMS, 2010.
// All values stay small here, so every result is exact.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/collatz-peak.epher"
// In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/collatz-peak.epher
// Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
//
// There is no knob: the starting values 27 and 13 are the checks.
// ============================================================================

// ---- one Collatz step ----------------------------------------------------------------
def step(n) = if mod(n, 2) == 0 then n / 2 else 3 * n + 1

// ---- flight of 27 -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// v = {current value, peak, steps}; the peak tracks the current value
// before each step, so the starting value counts.
v = {27, 27, 0}
while v[1] != 1 do v = {step(v[1]), max(v[2], step(v[1])), v[3] + 1}
print("27 peaks at:", v[2], "after", v[3], "steps")   // 9232, 111

// ---- flight of 13 -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// The same loop shape for a smaller start: 13 climbs to 40 then falls.
v = {13, 13, 0}
while v[1] != 1 do v = {step(v[1]), max(v[2], step(v[1])), v[3] + 1}
print("13 peaks at:", v[2], "after", v[3], "steps")   // 40, 9
// ---- expected output ----
// = {27, 27, 0}
// = 27 peaks at: 9232 after 111 steps
// = {13, 13, 0}
// = 13 peaks at: 40 after 9 steps
